#
#  Latest updates (v2.17.1):
#   • Chart heaviness root-caused and fixed on machines without a real GPU (owner report):
#     the terminal lines "Failed to create GLES3 context, fallback to GLES2" + "Failed to
#     create shared context for virtualization" are Chromium failing to get a REAL GPU context
#     — and since the v2.19.1 WebGL edition such a machine silently rendered EVERY chart frame
#     on the CPU (SwiftShader software WebGL), heavy exactly when those lines started printing.
#     The page now runs a GL gate once before any chart is built: a real hardware GL keeps the
#     WebGL edition untouched, while a software/no-GL machine gets null for every WebGL request
#     — the library's own silent identical Canvas 2D fallback then drives the chart (the same
#     fast path the 2D edition always used). Chromium flags now default to the HARDWARE GPU
#     path (--ignore-gpu-blocklist + --enable-gpu-rasterization; --use-angle=d3d11 on
#     Windows): on real-GPU machines the GPU context succeeds, so the GLES3 / shared-context
#     retry storm that made the chart heavy cannot start and those ERROR lines stop
#     printing. Only a screenless environment (BADI_SHOTS, or Linux without DISPLAY) takes
#     the software path (--disable-gpu + --enable-unsafe-swiftshader). The debug relay
#     (BADI::dbg / PAGEDBG) prints only in debug mode (BADI_SHOTS) — the normal terminal
#     stays quiet.
#   • Chart library updated: the CDN pin now loads BADI-CHARTS-V2 v2.19.1 chart-webgl.js (was
#     v2.19.0 chart.js + add-on) — in v2.19.1 the WebGL edition IS the full library in one file,
#     loaded INSTEAD of chart.js exactly as its loading contract requires (the old pair and its
#     chart.initWebGL() call are gone); the GL backend now activates automatically in the
#     constructor with a silent identical Canvas 2D fallback when no WebGL context exists,
#     every capture path still composites the internal GL canvas so screenshots keep the
#     candles, and --enable-unsafe-swiftshader keeps software WebGL working on no-GPU machines;
#     identical public API, verified against every call the app makes (user request)
#   • Badi Security Engine (BSE) — owner-requested independent permission system, four layers
#     built to fail independently (one failing never drops the whole defense):
#       Layer 1 — Script Sandbox: Python indicators already run with pinned safe builtins
#         (no import/files/network); JS indicators now run in a CLOSED scope — every escape
#         hatch (fetch, XMLHttpRequest, WebSocket, document, window, localStorage, Function,
#         Reflect, Proxy, WebAssembly, … 50+ names) is shadowed to undefined inside the
#         compiled script, so "typeof fetch" is "undefined" and the page is unreachable.
#       Layer 2 — API Validation / Network Broker: ALL network traffic now flows through one
#         broker (bse_open) — HTTPS only, exact host allow-list, every DNS answer must be a
#         PUBLIC routable address (loopback / private / link-local / 169.254.169.254 metadata
#         / reserved networks are rejected — a domain-name check alone is never trusted), the
#         connection is pinned to the validated IP while the TLS certificate is still verified
#         against the real hostname (DNS rebinding cannot switch the peer), every HTTP redirect
#         is re-validated against the same policy, system proxies are disabled, payload size
#         caps + sliding-window rate limits protect every script-initiated send (signals,
#         screenshots, composed images, Telegram total) and the console bridge got a flood
#         + size guard inside the page.
#       Layer 3 — OS Isolation: the studio page gets a strict Content-Security-Policy that
#         pins Chromium's network to the chart/editor CDN only — even code that escaped the
#         sandbox cannot open ANY connection to any other host (the engine itself rejects it).
#       Layer 4 — Core Protection: the bot token never enters the page; every blocked attempt
#         is audited to badi_security.log (no secrets, auto-rotated, unreadable to sandboxed
#         code which has no file access); repeated violations trigger the SECURITY HOLD —
#         all script-initiated sends stop until a clean restart of the app.
#       CPU budget: every Python indicator call (calculate / signal / get_range) runs
#         under a 3-second watchdog — a stuck pure-Python loop is killed mid-flight and
#         the violating indicator is auto-disabled (module removed, file switched off and
#         persisted — it stays off after a restart until the user re-enables it, stale
#         series removed from the chart); a slow-but-returned call gets the strike window
#         before the same auto-disable. (A bare-except loop that swallows the kill still
#         needs the planned separate-process engine — the budget covers the practical cases.)
#     The policy tables are code constants on purpose — no script and no config file can
#     edit them. Verified by probe_bse.py (blocked operations raise, allowed ones pass).
#   • Chart wears the app colors — always (owner request): the separate chart palette is gone.
#     Background, grid, candles, axes, crosshair and drawings are derived from the app theme
#     itself and re-applied live on every dark/light switch — even if custom chart colors were
#     changed from Settings before (old "chart_theme" values are ignored and dropped). The
#     Settings "Chart appearance" section is removed with it; Indicators.colors() from scripts
#     still works on top of the app theme ({reset:true} returns to it)
#   • Six UI languages (owner request): English, العربية, বাংলা, हिन्दी, Español
#     (Latin America) and اردو — every settings/console/message string translated,
#     Urdu joins Arabic in the RTL layout, and Indic/Nastaliq font fallbacks were
#     added so the new scripts render on every machine.
#   • Python programming guide (owner request): a full guide for the PYTHON language is now seeded
#     next to the JS one — BadiCharts_Python_Guide.py, 100% English, and it is itself a working
#     Bollinger indicator. It covers the whole Python engine: the .py indicator structure
#     (LABEL/TYPE/SETTINGS/calculate + get_range/signal), the short candle keys {"t","o","h","l","c","v"},
#     the injected helpers (sma/ema/rsi + math) and the safe builtins, overlay vs pane, the automatic
#     line drawing, Telegram signals (a chats id per indicator + automatic WIN/LOSS settlement), the
#     automatic chart screenshot on every signal (ONE message — the text rides on the photo), the
#     closed sandbox (no import/files/network — that is what keeps the device safe) and error
#     debugging. The JS guide got a new section 13 pointing to it
#   • Python sandbox hardened (found while writing the guide): exec() silently injects the REAL
#     builtins when the globals dict has no __builtins__ key — so "import os" and friends were
#     reachable from a Python indicator despite the safe-builtins list. The engine now pins
#     __builtins__ to the safe set explicitly: import/files/network are genuinely unreachable,
#     matching the closed-sandbox promise (verified by probe: import raises, open raises at call)
#   • Bot START/STOP switch on the Telegram page (owner request): with a token saved a running
#     switch appears — Start keeps the bot awake and sending everything (signals, WIN/LOSS
#     results, script screenshots, composed images, each as its ONE merged message); Stop halts
#     ALL Telegram sending until started again (signal cards and History results still work —
#     only the sending holds; the state is persisted in config.json and survives restarts;
#     the explicit Test button still works as the user's own diagnostic)
#   • Indicator labels always readable (owner report): some indicator names were drawn with the
#     indicator's own color — black text vanished on the dark theme and white text on the light
#     theme. Every indicator label color is now auto-fitted to the active theme (contrast-checked
#     against the pane background, the author's color kept for the drawing itself) and re-fitted
#     live on every dark/light switch — on all charts and all add paths
#   • Notifications card refined (owner report): the blue bell badge no longer touches the card
#     edge — the whole popup content is inset with proper padding, header and list breathe
#   • Free image composition from scripts (owner request): Indicators.compose() draws ANY picture on a
#     private off-screen canvas (a draw callback with the full Canvas 2D API — text, shapes, colors,
#     whatever the script wants — the chart library is not involved at all) and Indicators.composePy()
#     hands a JSON command list to the desktop, which composes the picture with Python/Qt QPainter
#     (fill/line/circle/text/polygon — a closed, safe command set, sharp Arabic/Unicode text) and
#     sends it to Telegram as ONE message (text on the photo as its caption). Nothing is captured
#     from the screen — the pixels are created by the script's own commands; nothing is written to
#     disk. Guide section 12
#   • Private virtual chart for scripts (owner request): Indicators.render() builds the script's OWN
#     chart off-screen — the last N candles (e.g. 20) or the script's own data, its own size, candle
#     width, colors, background and which indicators appear — captures it and sends it to Telegram
#     as ONE message (text on the photo), while the user's chart is never touched: no size change,
#     no zoom, no colors, nothing on screen. The virtual canvas is never attached to the page
#     (invisible by design) and is destroyed right after the capture; pixels come only from that
#     script-owned canvas — never the window, the editor, the desktop or the screen. Guide section 11
#   • Chart screenshots from scripts (owner request): Indicators.shot() lets an indicator capture
#     the chart itself and send it straight to Telegram — the script controls everything: the
#     whole pane, one pane or any region inside it (x/y/w/h), the candle width (candleW), which
#     indicators appear (hide/show), scale/format/quality — the text rides ON the photo as its
#     caption (ONE Telegram message, never a separate bubble) and nothing is saved to disk.
#     New Indicators.colors() gives scripts the candle colors (bull/bear + volumes) and the chart
#     background (bg/grid/axisText/crosshair/drawing). Hard security rule: the region is always
#     clamped to the chart canvas — a script can never capture the app window, the desktop or the
#     full screen (nothing outside the chart pixels can reach a script). Guide section 10 updated
#   • Buyer licensing moved into the indicator script (owner request): the seller lists the buyer
#     IDs inside the code — the BADI LICENSED USERS line, unlimited buyers, several lines allowed —
#     the encrypt dialog no longer holds any ID field: it only shows the buyers found in the script
#     before embedding them inside the encrypted file; the USER ID stays in the bottom bar (copy)
#   • Signals red badge = unread counter (owner request): it counts the signals that arrived while
#     the card was closed and clears to zero the moment the user opens the signals card
#   • Signals card design refined (owner request): a real title row (live dot + SIGNALS title +
#     language-aware), a segmented Signals/History tabs control, and more precise card details
#     (flag frame, uppercase row labels, direction-tinted top line)
#   • Programming guide: a full new section 8 explaining buyer licensing inside the script —
#     every step, every form of the marker line, unlimited buyers, re-encryption to change buyers
#   • The white strip right of the price axis is gone for good — root cause fixed: the chart
#     canvas stays CSS-driven so it always follows its container; opening the app in a smaller
#     window and maximizing it no longer freezes the chart at its first size (owner report)
#   • Editor UI now follows the app theme: the editor strip (Run/New/Save bar), the file tabs,
#     the empty-editor box and the new-file wizard card use theme variables instead of fixed
#     dark colors — switching dark/light recolors the whole editor area instantly
#   • English is the official documentation language: the programming guide file
